WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.164
A general assistant needs balanced instruction following, writing, knowledge, conversational behavior, and safe handling of uncertainty.

00:00:08.164 --> 00:00:17.260
A compact model may be sufficient for routine local help, while difficult analysis may justify a larger option.

00:00:17.260 --> 00:00:26.357
Coding assistants should be tested on the actual languages, repositories, context sizes, completion style, debugging, and tool workflow.

00:00:26.357 --> 00:00:32.655
Benchmark correctness and edit quality rather than relying on generic chat results.

00:00:32.655 --> 00:00:39.886
Writing and editing need tone control, preservation of meaning, revision quality, and consistent formatting.

00:00:39.886 --> 00:00:48.982
Cybersecurity analysis needs careful evidence handling, domain vocabulary, defensive framing, and low tolerance for invented indicators or commands.

00:00:48.982 --> 00:00:56.213
Document review and retrieval need source grounding, citation behavior, extraction accuracy, and long-context discipline.

00:00:56.213 --> 00:01:03.910
Private-assistant use adds requirements for logs, access, local integrations, and data retention beyond model quality.

00:01:03.910 --> 00:01:12.074
Vision-language workloads need image understanding, visual-token budget, OCR, counting, chart or screenshot performance, and additional memory.

00:01:12.074 --> 00:01:19.771
Automation and agents need tool selection, structured outputs, error recovery, and resistance to compounding mistakes.

00:01:19.771 --> 00:01:27.469
Select model families by demonstrated task fit, license, runtime support, language needs, and operational behavior.

00:01:27.469 --> 00:01:34.233
Maintain a fallback when one model is too slow, too weak, or unavailable.

00:01:34.233 --> 00:01:38.198
One model need not serve every workload.
